 taken with my  phone camera the polarizer square is  covering both the lens and the flash you  can see it with any iris but it's more  dramatic with blue and green eyes  anyway  patterns like this might help you notice  stuff you missed with your own eyes the  pattern in this protractor seems to be  radiating from an off-center point on  the edge a closer look reveals a tiny  chip in the plastic  something similar happens with my  triangle ruler with the polarizers you  can see a fine crack in the plastic it's  obvious the stress has been released at  that point which deformed the overall  pattern quite a bit due to the  redistribution of internal forces  engineers do this all the time to test  parts  of course if the rotation of that light  happened uniformly the ruler would be  solid white  that's not what happened though there's  a clear rainbow effect  the rotation the light experiences  doesn't just depend on the stress of the  material but also the wavelength of  light  you can actually see where the forces  are greatest within the plastic the same  thing happens inside your car windows  these spots are stress points within the  glass  points of maximum internal force  see there are two basic ways to make  glass annealing and tempering annealing  is pretty straightforward the glass is  molded and then allowed to cool  gradually  that means there are no particular  places within the glass where you see an  excess of stress  front wheel front wheel shields  [Laughter]  front wheels  i can do this  the front windshields of vehicles are  made with annealed glass and so are the  windows in your home  tempered glass is different  the glass is still molded but it's  cooled off quickly from the outside  which distributes the internal forces  unevenly we end up with high tension in  some places and high compression in  others  why would anyone do that  safety safety safety  if you ever end up with your car in a  body of water you're going to be happy  with this design